    keywords: "tour operator website Dharamshala, Spiti tour website, motorcycle tour website India, travel website design system, sister site SEO, TouristTrip schema, WhatsApp travel enquiry, Astro travel website, Divyansh Sood",
    feature: { src: "/images/work/himalayan-brothers-1.webp", alt: "Himalayan Brothers Travel — homepage hero, desktop" },
    body: [
      { tag: "The brief", title: "From a parked domain", type: "prose",
        html: `himalayanbrothers.com was a parked domain with a placeholder landing page. The family wanted it to sell what they already do from Buddha House: tours, taxis, treks and bike hire. The inputs were a <strong>49-page mockup</strong> with the itineraries roughed out, and the design-system document written while building <a href="/projects/hotel-himalayan-brothers/">the hotel group's site</a>, which had been written as a handoff for exactly this. The site went live on 25 October 2026.` },
      { tag: "Stack", type: "list", items: ["Astro 7, static output", "Design tokens shared with the hotel site", "axe-core WCAG 2.2 gate", "PageSpeed Insights gate", "TouristTrip and TaxiService schema"] },
      { tag: "Sibling", title: "Same family, same system", type: "prose",
        html: `The design tokens are copied from the hotel site unchanged, local additions live in one separate stylesheet, and the components follow the same rules. There's one agreed exception: the home hero's headline is allowed to grow past the hotel site's heading cap. The logo stays the family's logo. The design system forbids adding “Travels” to the mark, so the word goes in the text instead. A guest moving from a hotel page to a tour shouldn't feel they've changed websites, and they don't.` },
      { tag: "Two sites", title: "Two sites, one graph", type: "prose",
        html: `Hotel cards link to each hotel's own page, “Our hotels” sits in the menu and footer, and the footer says <em>Book direct at hotelhimalayanbrothers.com</em>. Underneath, the travel site's structured data doesn't re-describe the hotels. It points at the hotel site's own <code>Hotel</code> IDs, so search engines and AI assistants see one family business with a hotel arm and a travel desk, rather than two strangers that happen to share a phone number. The family's third site, <a href="/projects/sach-pass/">sachpass.com</a>, shares the same brand files and covers the road over Sach Pass.` },
      { tag: "What we shipped", title: "Fifteen trips, eight roads", type: "prose",
        html: `<strong>15 tour packages</strong>, from a short Dharamshala escape to an 8-day Spiti circuit from Shimla to Manali, the Indrahar Pass trek, a winter snow-leopard trail and an 18-day motorcycle loop. Three of the 15 are motorcycle tours. There are <strong>8 taxi route pages</strong> (Kangra Airport to Dharamshala, Chamba to Killar over Sach Pass, Manali to Kaza and more), 20 experiences, a bike-rental page, three destination guides and 12 blog posts. The rule for the taxi pages was “never a copy with the names swapped”: each says what is actually different about its road.` },
      { tag: "Enquiries", title: "Every button writes a message", type: "prose",
        html: `There is no payment and no booking engine. Every call to action builds a WhatsApp message for its context: a trip, a taxi fare, an activity, or a plain question. There's a share-to-a-friend link for group planning, and a plain-link fallback when JavaScript is off. A trip planner collects dates, people and interests into one message. Prices say “quote with your itinerary” until the family confirms them.` },
      { tag: "Honesty", title: "Drafted, and saying so", type: "prose",
        html: `The mockup supplied itineraries the family hadn't checked yet. So all 15 tours carry a <code>needsClientReview</code> flag, and a content report lists every drafted text, unpriced item and open decision. A “last reviewed by the travel desk” line was removed until someone actually has. “24/7 on WhatsApp” was softened to “24/7 help on the road”, because the copy contradicted itself. There are no trip reviews yet, so the site shows the five hotels' Google ratings, labelled as hotel ratings, and the trip-review list stays empty rather than borrowed.` },
      { tag: "Dates", title: "One date, three places", type: "prose",
        html: `A single module holds each page's last-modified date. It drives the sitemap's <code>lastmod</code>, the <code>dateModified</code> in the page's structured data, and the visible “Last updated” line. They can't disagree, which is the whole point. A sitemap that claims every page changed today teaches search engines to ignore its dates.` },
      { tag: "Gates", title: "Checks that look at the phone, not just the code", type: "prose",
        html: `The accessibility script runs axe against WCAG 2.2 A and AA at 390 and 1440 pixels wide, and fails on any violation, any sideways scroll, a fixed bar covering more than a fifth of the screen, or text under 12 px. The speed script runs PageSpeed Insights on each page type and fails under 90, on LCP over 2.5 seconds, or on layout shift over 0.1. The SEO audit adds that every FAQ question in the structured data must also be visible on the page. At launch: zero type errors, zero SEO issues, zero axe violations, and Lighthouse mobile performance of 98–100.` },
      { tag: "The lesson", type: "prose",
        html: `Build a sister site from the first site's system, not its screenshots. Copied tokens match exactly; a look matched by eye never quite lands. And a site full of drafts can still launch honestly, as long as it says which parts are drafts.` },
    ],
