    stackLine: "Astro 7 · TypeScript · cheerio crawl · Lighthouse and 360 px gates · Vercel",
    service: { href: "/services/travel-website-development/", label: "Travel & taxi website development" },
    title: "Leisure Himachal — Manali Travel Agency Website · Divyansh Sood",
    description: "A Manali travel agency's 2011 site rebuilt in Astro: 96 old pages crawled, 71 redirects, 77 new pages, every unconfirmed fact flagged for the owner.",
    ogTitle: "Leisure Himachal — Manali travel agency website · Case study",
    ogDescription: "A 15-year-old travel site, read in full, then rebuilt: 71 redirects and every unconfirmed fact marked.",
    ogImage: "https://www.divyanshsood.com/images/og/leisure-himachal.jpg",
    keywords: "Manali travel agency website, Himachal tour packages website, Leh Ladakh tour website, website migration redirects, old website redesign, travel website developer Manali, Wayback Machine migration, Astro travel website, Divyansh Sood",
    feature: { src: "/images/work/leisure-himachal-1.webp", alt: "Leisure Himachal — homepage hero, desktop" },
    body: [
      { tag: "The brief", title: "Fifteen years of a website", type: "prose",
        html: `Leisure Himachal sells fixed packages across Himachal and Leh-Ladakh from Manali: Volvo trips from Delhi, private-car tours, jeep safaris to Leh and Srinagar, plus taxis and hotel bookings. Its site was hand-built HTML from 2011. It had no canonical tags, no Open Graph, no structured data and no sitemap, and most pages shared one title. The 12 package pages had no meta descriptions, the photos were 240 × 100 pixels, and analytics still ran Universal Analytics, which stopped collecting in July 2023. The owner's own playbook puts the starting point plainly: <em>“Today, a web search for ‘Leisure Himachal’ finds nothing at all.”</em>` },
      { tag: "Stack", type: "list", items: ["Astro 7, static output", "TypeScript (strict)", "cheerio for the crawl, sharp for photos", "One self-hosted Inter file", "Lighthouse and 360 px mobile gates"] },
      { tag: "The crawl", title: "Read every version before rewriting any", type: "prose",
        html: `A crawler took the <strong>22 live pages and 74 Wayback Machine captures</strong> from 2011 to 2026, as raw HTML and as text, with an inventory of every image. The old site contradicted itself: three different prices for most packages, three bank accounts, two cancellation policies, and two package codes each used twice. So nothing was carried over blindly. A facts file traces every business detail to where it was found and which version is believed. One trip had three altitudes for the same pass (4,111, 3,979 and 3,980 m), now 3,978 m everywhere, and a misspelt border became Wagah.` },
      { tag: "Unconfirmed", title: "Unconfirmed, and visibly so", type: "prose",
        html: `Some facts only the owner can settle: prices, the office address and hours, bank details, the cancellation policy, the registration number. They're asked as numbered questions, and every unconfirmed fact on the site is wrapped in a component that carries its question number. Add <code>#review</code> to any URL and each one is <strong>outlined in orange with its question number</strong>, so the owner can walk the site and see exactly what's waiting on them. The release audit fails while any remain.` },
      { tag: "Redirects", title: "Every old address has somewhere to go", type: "prose",
        html: `A script generates <strong>71 redirect rules</strong> into both the redirects file and the Vercel config, and refuses a duplicate source. The audit then checks every legacy URL in the crawl inventory and fails if any has neither a page nor a redirect. Pages with no honest new home go on a GONE list and are allowed to 404. Redirecting them all to the home page would look tidy, but Google treats that as a soft 404 anyway.` },
      { tag: "Prices", title: "One price, and a code that isn't on the page", type: "prose",
        html: `Each package shows a single “from ₹” price. The full tariff tables are kept in the content files but never rendered, and the audit fails if a structured-data price differs from the visible one. The prices themselves are still among the owner's open questions. The standing 10% offer works the other way round: <strong>the code is never printed</strong>. Every form has a “send me my 10% code” box ticked by default, the agent replies with it, and the audit fails if the code appears anywhere in the build.` },
      { tag: "Pass calendar", title: "The passes, dated and sourced", type: "prose",
        html: `Half the questions a Manali agency gets are about roads. The site keeps about two dozen dated, sourced records for Rohtang, the Atal Tunnel, Kunzum and Baralacha, which is the kind of answer a traveller and an AI assistant can both quote. The audit warns when the calendar is more than 120 days old. A linter requires any itinerary day that names Rohtang, Sarchu, Manikaran, Khajjiar, McLeodganj or the Volvo to link the guide that explains it.` },
      { tag: "Photos", title: "Photos that match the page", type: "prose",
        html: `The mockup came with AI-rendered scenery; none of it was used. The site runs on 72 photos: 64 from Wikimedia Commons, every one credited on a photo-credits page that every page links to, plus the agency's own. A content linter checks that a package's hero shows a place on its route, that no two packages share a hero, that destination galleries stay in their region, and that every master is at least 1,600 px wide. One script grades them all to the same look from untouched originals, so the grade can be redone.` },
      { tag: "Phone-first", title: "Built for a thumb", type: "prose",
        html: `There is no <code>:hover</code> rule anywhere in the CSS, and the audit fails if one appears. A 360 px check fails on sideways scroll, running text under 16 px, tap targets under 44 px, or fixed bars covering more than a quarter of the screen. Lighthouse must score <strong>95 or more on mobile across 22 pages covering 16 templates</strong>. One font file, 48 KB. The rupee sign had been quietly pulling in an extra 83 KB file, until it was fixed. The mockup's search bar became a trip composer — its own code calls it <em>“the mockup's search bar, made honest”</em> — which writes a WhatsApp message instead of pretending to search inventory.` },
      { tag: "Off the website", title: "The other half of getting found", type: "prose",
        html: `A website alone doesn't put a Manali agency in front of travellers. Map listings come first, and AI assistants only name a business when its site, listing and directories agree. So the handover included an off-site playbook for the owner: answer the open questions first, claim the Google Business Profile under the exact name, list the packages as products, ask for reviews steadily and never with incentives, and ask ChatGPT, Perplexity and Gemini the same eight questions each month to see what changes.` },
      { tag: "The lesson", type: "prose",
        html: `Before rewriting an old site, read every version of it. Fifteen years of a business are in there, along with three prices for the same trip, and only the owner can say which one is true. For what a Himachal site costs to build, see <a href="/blog/website-cost-himachal-pradesh/">website cost in Himachal Pradesh</a>.` },
    ],
